test-net-server-reset.js: node-lane binding artifact; the server resets both accepted sockets the instant the second one connects, and the second client loses that race here for a reason outside node:net's algorithm. Node's `internalConnectMultiple` issues `handle.connect()` synchronously, while its single-address path defers an IP literal through `process.nextTick`; this profile reaches connect(2) through the host `net.connect` of `bindings.node.mjs`, which always takes that deferred path, so every attempt is one tick late and the RST arrives first. Measured both ways: with `autoSelectFamily:false` the second connect fails as a bare `connect ECONNRESET` and the file passed on the code alone while never reproducing node's sequence, and with node's default auto-select-family it retries the second address, that attempt is refused because the server has already closed, and node's own aggregation rule reports an AggregateError. The tick does not exist in the compiled lane.
test-blocklist.js: language non-goal (§13); two of its blocks require util.inspect metadata this profile refuses -- the depth-exceeded marker carrying a constructor's name (`[BlockList]`, where ours is `[Object]`) and node's symbol-keyed util.inspect.custom hook rendering `rules: []`. Everything else in the file, which is nearly all of it, is retained unchanged in local/blocklist-static.js: the address and subnet rules, every ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE, isBlockList, and the whole JSON round trip
test-blocklist-clone.js: private serialization internals; it posts a BlockList through a MessagePort and requires the clone to share the original's native handle, so that adding an address to one is visible through the other. Node does that with the internal kClone/kDeserialize symbol protocol over a handle the two copies share; this profile's BlockList holds ordinary typed state and structured clone has no route to it
